              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nClient.login() calls FTPContext.send() from inside the control-socket data event handler. send() throws on CR, LF or NUL, and nothing catches the throw. A password containing \\r\\n therefore raises an uncaught exception and terminates the whole Node.js process. It does not reject the returned promise. Any application that passes untrusted input as the FTP password can be crashed remotely with one request. The same pattern exists in the REST/RETR path of downloadTo() when the remote path contains control characters.\n\n### Details\n\nThe CRLF guard (FTPContext.send, src/FtpContext.ts ~L233) correctly refuses to send commands containing \\r, \\n or \\0:\n```ts\nsend(command: string) {\n    if (/[\\r\\n\\0]/.test(command)) {\n        throw new Error(`Invalid command: Contains control characters. (${command})`);\n    }\n```\n\nThat works when send() is called from handle(), because it runs inside a Promise executor and the throw becomes a rejection. But in two places send() runs inside a response handler, which is called synchronously from the socket’s data event:\n\nClient.login() (src/Client.ts ~L246-248): after the server answers 331 to USER, the handler calls this.ftp.send(\"PASS \" + password). The USER command was already sent and is valid, so the password is only checked at this point. The call chain is socket.on(\"data\") → _onControlSocketData → _passToHandler → responseHandler → send() → throw. No try/catch exists anywhere in that chain, so it becomes an uncaughtException.\ndownloadTo() (src/transfer.ts ~L368-370): when startAt \u003e 0, the initial command is REST n, which contains no user-controlled text. When the server answers 350, the handler calls config.ftp.send(\"RETR \" + config.remotePath). This remotePath was never checked before. A path with a control character throws in the data handler in the same way. (Found by code reading, not reproduced.)\n\nBecause the exception happens during data event dispatch, it can’t be handled with try/catch or .catch() around client.access() or downloadTo(). The application only survives if it has a global process.on(\"uncaughtException\") handler.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nTested on Node.js v22.22.0 against basic-ftp 6.2.2 (built from source). It uses a minimal mock FTP server on localhost:\n\n```js\nconst net = require(\"net\")\nconst ftp = require(\"basic-ftp\")\n\nconst srv = net.createServer(s =\u003e {\n  s.write(\"220 hi\\r\\n\")\n  s.on(\"data\", d =\u003e {\n    const c = d.toString()\n    if (c.startsWith(\"USER\")) s.write(\"331 pw\\r\\n\")\n    else if (c.startsWith(\"PASS\")) s.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n    else s.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n  })\n})\n\nsrv.listen(0, async () =\u003e {\n  const c = new ftp.Client(2000)\n  try {\n    await c.access({\n      host: \"127.0.0.1\",\n      port: srv.address().port,\n      user: \"bob\",\n      password: \"x\\r\\nDELE important\"   // attacker-controlled value\n    })\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log(\"rejected:\", e.message)  // never reached\n  }\n  console.log(\"handled gracefully\")      // never reached\n  process.exit(0)\n})\n```\nResult: the process terminates with an uncaught exception and neither catch nor the final console.log runs:\n```\nError: Invalid command: Contains control characters. (PASS x\nDELE important)\n    at FTPContext.send (dist/FtpContext.js:189:19)\n    at Object.responseHandler (dist/Client.js:186:26)\n    at FTPContext._passToHandler (dist/FtpContext.js:322:24)\n    at FTPContext._onControlSocketData (dist/FtpContext.js:312:18)\n    at Socket.\u003canonymous\u003e (dist/FtpContext.js:133:44)\n```\nThe server in the PoC is benign. Any normal FTP server that replies 331 to USER triggers the same crash.\n\n### Impact\n\nDenial of service (uncaught exception, process termination), CWE-248.\n\nIt affects applications that pass untrusted or user-influenced values as the FTP password, or as the remote path of a resumed download. Examples are web apps or services that let users enter their own FTP credentials, or file-transfer or backup tools driven by user-supplied paths. One request crashes the whole Node.js process and every other client it serves. It needs no authentication to the FTP server and no special server behaviour. The attacker only needs control over the string. Confidentiality and integrity are not affected, and the CRLF guard still blocks command injection.\n\nSuggested fix:\n\nIn FTPContext._passToHandler(), wrap the handler call in a try/catch that rejects the task and calls closeWithError(err).\nValidate password in login(), and remotePath in _downloadToStream(), before any I/O and reject early.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nClient.login() calls FTPContext.send() from inside the control-socket data event handler. send() throws on CR, LF or NUL, and nothing catches the throw. A password containing \\r\\n therefore raises an uncaught exception and terminates the whole Node.js process. It does not reject the returned promise. Any application that passes untrusted input as the FTP password can be crashed remotely with one request. The same pattern exists in the REST/RETR path of downloadTo() when the remote path contains control characters.\n\n### Details\n\nThe CRLF guard (FTPContext.send, src/FtpContext.ts ~L233) correctly refuses to send commands containing \\r, \\n or \\0:\n```ts\nsend(command: string) {\n    if (/[\\r\\n\\0]/.test(command)) {\n        throw new Error(`Invalid command: Contains control characters. (${command})`);\n    }\n```\n\nThat works when send() is called from handle(), because it runs inside a Promise executor and the throw becomes a rejection. But in two places send() runs inside a response handler, which is called synchronously from the socket’s data event:\n\nClient.login() (src/Client.ts ~L246-248): after the server answers 331 to USER, the handler calls this.ftp.send(\"PASS \" + password). The USER command was already sent and is valid, so the password is only checked at this point. The call chain is socket.on(\"data\") → _onControlSocketData → _passToHandler → responseHandler → send() → throw. No try/catch exists anywhere in that chain, so it becomes an uncaughtException.\ndownloadTo() (src/transfer.ts ~L368-370): when startAt \u003e 0, the initial command is REST n, which contains no user-controlled text. When the server answers 350, the handler calls config.ftp.send(\"RETR \" + config.remotePath). This remotePath was never checked before. A path with a control character throws in the data handler in the same way. (Found by code reading, not reproduced.)\n\nBecause the exception happens during data event dispatch, it can’t be handled with try/catch or .catch() around client.access() or downloadTo(). The application only survives if it has a global process.on(\"uncaughtException\") handler.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nTested on Node.js v22.22.0 against basic-ftp 6.2.2 (built from source). It uses a minimal mock FTP server on localhost:\n\n```js\nconst net = require(\"net\")\nconst ftp = require(\"basic-ftp\")\n\nconst srv = net.createServer(s =\u003e {\n  s.write(\"220 hi\\r\\n\")\n  s.on(\"data\", d =\u003e {\n    const c = d.toString()\n    if (c.startsWith(\"USER\")) s.write(\"331 pw\\r\\n\")\n    else if (c.startsWith(\"PASS\")) s.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n    else s.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n  })\n})\n\nsrv.listen(0, async () =\u003e {\n  const c = new ftp.Client(2000)\n  try {\n    await c.access({\n      host: \"127.0.0.1\",\n      port: srv.address().port,\n      user: \"bob\",\n      password: \"x\\r\\nDELE important\"   // attacker-controlled value\n    })\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log(\"rejected:\", e.message)  // never reached\n  }\n  console.log(\"handled gracefully\")      // never reached\n  process.exit(0)\n})\n```\nResult: the process terminates with an uncaught exception and neither catch nor the final console.log runs:\n```\nError: Invalid command: Contains control characters. (PASS x\nDELE important)\n    at FTPContext.send (dist/FtpContext.js:189:19)\n    at Object.responseHandler (dist/Client.js:186:26)\n    at FTPContext._passToHandler (dist/FtpContext.js:322:24)\n    at FTPContext._onControlSocketData (dist/FtpContext.js:312:18)\n    at Socket.\u003canonymous\u003e (dist/FtpContext.js:133:44)\n```\nThe server in the PoC is benign. Any normal FTP server that replies 331 to USER triggers the same crash.\n\n### Impact\n\nDenial of service (uncaught exception, process termination), CWE-248.\n\nIt affects applications that pass untrusted or user-influenced values as the FTP password, or as the remote path of a resumed download. Examples are web apps or services that let users enter their own FTP credentials, or file-transfer or backup tools driven by user-supplied paths. One request crashes the whole Node.js process and every other client it serves. It needs no authentication to the FTP server and no special server behaviour. The attacker only needs control over the string. Confidentiality and integrity are not affected, and the CRLF guard still blocks command injection.\n\nSuggested fix:\n\nIn FTPContext._passToHandler(), wrap the handler call in a try/catch that rejects the task and calls closeWithError(err).\nValidate password in login(), and remotePath in _downloadToStream(), before any I/O and reject early."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-126346](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-126346)**.\n\n## [GHSA-r4jh-29pc-gfqr](https://osv.dev/GHSA-r4jh-29pc-gfqr)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Client.login() calls FTPContext.send() from inside the control-socket data event handler. send() throws on CR, LF or NUL, and nothing catches the throw. A password containing \\r\\n therefore raises an uncaught exception and terminates the whole Node.js process. It does not reject the returned promise. Any application that passes untrusted input as the FTP password can be crashed remotely with one request. The same pattern exists in the REST/RETR path of downloadTo() when the remote path contains control characters.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The CRLF guard (FTPContext.send, src/FtpContext.ts ~L233) correctly refuses to send commands containing \\r, \\n or \\0:\n\u003e ```ts\n\u003e send(command: string) {\n\u003e     if (/[\\r\\n\\0]/.test(command)) {\n\u003e         throw new Error(`Invalid command: Contains control characters. (${command})`);\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e That works when send() is called from handle(), because it runs inside a Promise executor and the throw becomes a rejection. But in two places send() runs inside a response handler, which is called synchronously from the socket’s data event:\n\u003e \n\u003e Client.login() (src/Client.ts ~L246-248): after the server answers 331 to USER, the handler calls this.ftp.send(\"PASS \" + password). The USER command was already sent and is valid, so the password is only checked at this point. The call chain is socket.on(\"data\") → _onControlSocketData → _passToHandler → responseHandler → send() → throw. No try/catch exists anywhere in that chain, so it becomes an uncaughtException.\n\u003e downloadTo() (src/transfer.ts ~L368-370): when startAt \u003e 0, the initial command is REST n, which contains no user-controlled text. When the server answers 350, the handler calls config.ftp.send(\"RETR \" + config.remotePath). This remotePath was never checked before. A path with a control character throws in the data handler in the same way. (Found by code reading, not reproduced.)\n\u003e \n\u003e Because the exception happens during data event dispatch, it can’t be handled with try/catch or .catch() around client.access() or downloadTo(). The application only survives if it has a global process.on(\"uncaughtException\") handler.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested on Node.js v22.22.0 against basic-ftp 6.2.2 (built from source). It uses a minimal mock FTP server on localhost:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const net = require(\"net\")\n\u003e const ftp = require(\"basic-ftp\")\n\u003e \n\u003e const srv = net.createServer(s =\u003e {\n\u003e   s.write(\"220 hi\\r\\n\")\n\u003e   s.on(\"data\", d =\u003e {\n\u003e     const c = d.toString()\n\u003e     if (c.startsWith(\"USER\")) s.write(\"331 pw\\r\\n\")\n\u003e     else if (c.startsWith(\"PASS\")) s.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e     else s.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e   })\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e srv.listen(0, async () =\u003e {\n\u003e   const c = new ftp.Client(2000)\n\u003e   try {\n\u003e     await c.access({\n\u003e       host: \"127.0.0.1\",\n\u003e       port: srv.address().port,\n\u003e       user: \"bob\",\n\u003e       password: \"x\\r\\nDELE important\"   // attacker-controlled value\n\u003e     })\n\u003e   } catch (e) {\n\u003e     console.log(\"rejected:\", e.message)  // never reached\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   console.log(\"handled gracefully\")      // never reached\n\u003e   process.exit(0)\n\u003e })\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Result: the process terminates with an uncaught exception and neither catch nor the final console.log runs:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Error: Invalid command: Contains control characters. (PASS x\n\u003e DELE important)\n\u003e     at FTPContext.send (dist/FtpContext.js:189:19)\n\u003e     at Object.responseHandler (dist/Client.js:186:26)\n\u003e     at FTPContext._passToHandler (dist/FtpContext.js:322:24)\n\u003e     at FTPContext._onControlSocketData (dist/FtpContext.js:312:18)\n\u003e     at Socket.\u003canonymous\u003e (dist/FtpContext.js:133:44)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The server in the PoC is benign. Any normal FTP server that replies 331 to USER triggers the same crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Denial of service (uncaught exception, process termination), CWE-248.\n\u003e \n\u003e It affects applications that pass untrusted or user-influenced values as the FTP password, or as the remote path of a resumed download. Examples are web apps or services that let users enter their own FTP credentials, or file-transfer or backup tools driven by user-supplied paths. One request crashes the whole Node.js process and every other client it serves. It needs no authentication to the FTP server and no special server behaviour. The attacker only needs control over the string. Confidentiality and integrity are not affected, and the CRLF guard still blocks command injection.\n\u003e \n\u003e Suggested fix:\n\u003e \n\u003e In FTPContext._passToHandler(), wrap the handler call in a try/catch that rejects the task and calls closeWithError(err).\n\u003e Validate password in login(), and remotePath in _downloadToStream(), before any I/O and reject early.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/package-lock.json | basic-ftp | 5.3.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-r4jh-29pc-gfqr | basic-ftp | 6.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-126346\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-126346](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-126346)**.\n\n## [GHSA-r4jh-29pc-gfqr](https://osv.dev/GHSA-r4jh-29pc-gfqr)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Client.login() calls FTPContext.send() from inside the control-socket data event handler. send() throws on CR, LF or NUL, and nothing catches the throw. A password containing \\r\\n therefore raises an uncaught exception and terminates the whole Node.js process. It does not reject the returned promise. Any application that passes untrusted input as the FTP password can be crashed remotely with one request. The same pattern exists in the REST/RETR path of downloadTo() when the remote path contains control characters.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e The CRLF guard (FTPContext.send, src/FtpContext.ts ~L233) correctly refuses to send commands containing \\r, \\n or \\0:\n\u003e ```ts\n\u003e send(command: string) {\n\u003e     if (/[\\r\\n\\0]/.test(command)) {\n\u003e         throw new Error(`Invalid command: Contains control characters. (${command})`);\n\u003e     }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e That works when send() is called from handle(), because it runs inside a Promise executor and the throw becomes a rejection. But in two places send() runs inside a response handler, which is called synchronously from the socket’s data event:\n\u003e \n\u003e Client.login() (src/Client.ts ~L246-248): after the server answers 331 to USER, the handler calls this.ftp.send(\"PASS \" + password). The USER command was already sent and is valid, so the password is only checked at this point. The call chain is socket.on(\"data\") → _onControlSocketData → _passToHandler → responseHandler → send() → throw. No try/catch exists anywhere in that chain, so it becomes an uncaughtException.\n\u003e downloadTo() (src/transfer.ts ~L368-370): when startAt \u003e 0, the initial command is REST n, which contains no user-controlled text. When the server answers 350, the handler calls config.ftp.send(\"RETR \" + config.remotePath). This remotePath was never checked before. A path with a control character throws in the data handler in the same way. (Found by code reading, not reproduced.)\n\u003e \n\u003e Because the exception happens during data event dispatch, it can’t be handled with try/catch or .catch() around client.access() or downloadTo(). The application only survives if it has a global process.on(\"uncaughtException\") handler.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Tested on Node.js v22.22.0 against basic-ftp 6.2.2 (built from source). It uses a minimal mock FTP server on localhost:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const net = require(\"net\")\n\u003e const ftp = require(\"basic-ftp\")\n\u003e \n\u003e const srv = net.createServer(s =\u003e {\n\u003e   s.write(\"220 hi\\r\\n\")\n\u003e   s.on(\"data\", d =\u003e {\n\u003e     const c = d.toString()\n\u003e     if (c.startsWith(\"USER\")) s.write(\"331 pw\\r\\n\")\n\u003e     else if (c.startsWith(\"PASS\")) s.write(\"230 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e     else s.write(\"200 ok\\r\\n\")\n\u003e   })\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e srv.listen(0, async () =\u003e {\n\u003e   const c = new ftp.Client(2000)\n\u003e   try {\n\u003e     await c.access({\n\u003e       host: \"127.0.0.1\",\n\u003e       port: srv.address().port,\n\u003e       user: \"bob\",\n\u003e       password: \"x\\r\\nDELE important\"   // attacker-controlled value\n\u003e     })\n\u003e   } catch (e) {\n\u003e     console.log(\"rejected:\", e.message)  // never reached\n\u003e   }\n\u003e   console.log(\"handled gracefully\")      // never reached\n\u003e   process.exit(0)\n\u003e })\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Result: the process terminates with an uncaught exception and neither catch nor the final console.log runs:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e Error: Invalid command: Contains control characters. (PASS x\n\u003e DELE important)\n\u003e     at FTPContext.send (dist/FtpContext.js:189:19)\n\u003e     at Object.responseHandler (dist/Client.js:186:26)\n\u003e     at FTPContext._passToHandler (dist/FtpContext.js:322:24)\n\u003e     at FTPContext._onControlSocketData (dist/FtpContext.js:312:18)\n\u003e     at Socket.\u003canonymous\u003e (dist/FtpContext.js:133:44)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e The server in the PoC is benign. Any normal FTP server that replies 331 to USER triggers the same crash.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Denial of service (uncaught exception, process termination), CWE-248.\n\u003e \n\u003e It affects applications that pass untrusted or user-influenced values as the FTP password, or as the remote path of a resumed download. Examples are web apps or services that let users enter their own FTP credentials, or file-transfer or backup tools driven by user-supplied paths. One request crashes the whole Node.js process and every other client it serves. It needs no authentication to the FTP server and no special server behaviour. The attacker only needs control over the string. Confidentiality and integrity are not affected, and the CRLF guard still blocks command injection.\n\u003e \n\u003e Suggested fix:\n\u003e \n\u003e In FTPContext._passToHandler(), wrap the handler call in a try/catch that rejects the task and calls closeWithError(err).\n\u003e Validate password in login(), and remotePath in _downloadToStream(), before any I/O and reject early.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/home/user/felipe/speckit/juice-shop-master/package-lock.json | basic-ftp | 5.3.1 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-r4jh-29pc-gfqr | basic-ftp | 6.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them,